==== Ur III Ziggurat

During the Ur III period, Ur-Nammu had a ziggurat built over the remains of
previous temples. Aside from [[ Enmerkar ]] of Uruk (as mentioned in the
//Aratta// epics), several later historical Sumerian kings are said in
inscriptions found here to have ordered improvement or re-building works on
the //e-abzu// temple, including [[ Elili | elulu ]] of Ur; [[ Ur-Nammu ]],
[[ Shulgi ]] and [[ Amar-Sin ]] of [[ Ur-III ]], and [[ Nur-Adad ]] of
[[ Larsa ]].[[ ^36 ]][[ ^37 ]]

== History

=== Chalcolithic Age

==== Ubaid period

Eridu is one of the earliest settlements in the region, founded
[[ c. 5400 BC | 6th-millennium-bc ]] during the early [[ Ubaid period ]], at
that time close to the [[ Persian Gulf ]] near the mouth of the
[[ Euphrates ]], although in modern times it is about 90 miles inland.
Excavation has shown that the city was founded on a virgin sand dune site with
no previous habitation. Eighteen superimposed mudbrick temples at the site
(see above, section "Architecture") underlie the unfinished [[ ziggurat ]] of
[[ Amar-Sin ]] (c. 2047–2039 BC). Levels XIX to VI were from the Ubaid period
and Levels V to I were dated to the [[ Uruk period ]].[[ ^38 ]]

During the [[ Ubaid period ]] the site extended out to an area of about 12
hectares (about 30 acres). Twelve neolithic clay tokens, the precursor to
[[ Proto-cuneiform ]], were found in the Ubaid levels of the
site.[[ ^39 ]][[ ^40 ]][[ ^41 ]]

<ul
  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Bowl_MET_DP104229.jpg/120px-Bowl_MET_DP104229.jpg" alt="Bowl; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; 6.99 cm; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan Museum of Art" height="120" width="120"

    Bowl; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; [[ ceramic ]]; 6.99cm; Tell Abu Shahrain;
    [[ Metropolitan Museum of Art ]]

  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Bowl_MET_DP104228_%28cropped%29.jpg/120px-Bowl_MET_DP104228_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Bowl excavated in the Ubaid Cemetery at Eridu (Grave 134)" height="64" width="120"

    Bowl excavated in the Ubaid Cemetery at Eridu (Grave 134)

  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Bowl_MET_DP104227.jpg/120px-Bowl_MET_DP104227.jpg" alt="Bowl; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan Museum of Art" height="120" width="120"

    Bowl; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan
    Museum of Art

  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Cup_MET_ME49_133_5.jpg/120px-Cup_MET_ME49_133_5.jpg" alt="Cup; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; 8.56 cm; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan Museum of Art" height="120" width="107"

    Cup; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; 8.56cm; Tell Abu Shahrain;
    Metropolitan Museum of Art

==== Uruk period

Significant habitation was found from the Uruk period with "non-secular"
buildings being found in soundings. Uruk finds included decorative terracotta
cones topped with copper, copper nails topped with gold, a pair of basalt
stone lion statues, columns several meters in diameter coated with cones and
gypsum, and extensive Uruk period pottery.[[ ^42 ]][[ ^43 ]][[ ^44 ]][[ ^45 ]]

=== Early Bronze Age

==== Early Dynastic Period

Occupation increased in the Early Dynastic period with a monumental 100 m
× 100 m (330 ft × 330 ft) palace being constructed.[[ ^46 ]] In Early Dynastic
IIIA (c. 2750/2600-2500 BCE), an inscription of [[ Elulu ]], a ruler of the
[[ First Dynasty of Ur ]] (c. 2600 BC), was found at Eridu.[[ ^47 ]] In Early
Dynastic IIIB (c. 2500-2350 BCE), a statue of the ruler [[ Entemena ]] of
[[ Lagash ]] (c. 2400 BCE) reads: "he built Ab-zupasira for Enki, king of
Eridu \...".[[ ^48 ]]

According to the excavators, construction of the
[[ Ur III | third-dynasty-of-ur ]] ziggurat and associated buildings was
preceded by the destruction of preceding construction and its use as leveling
fill so no remains from that time were found. At a small mound 1 kilometer
north of Eridu two Early Dynastic III palaces were found, with an enclosure
wall. The palaces measured 45 meters by 65 meters with 2.6 meter wide walls
and were constructed in the standard Early Dynastic period method of
plano-convex bricks laid in a herringbone fashion.[[ ^17 ]]

<ul
  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Painted_pottery_vessel_%28jug_or_ewer%29._From_Eridu_%28Tell_Abu_Shahrain%29%2C_Iraq._3500-2800_BCE._Iraq_Museum%2C_Baghdad.jpg/120px-Painted_pottery_vessel_%28jug_or_ewer%29._From_Eridu_%28Tell_Abu_Shahrain%29%2C_Iraq._3500-2800_BCE._Iraq_Museum%2C_Baghdad.jpg" height="90" width="120"

==== Akkadian period

In the Akkadian period (c. 2350-2150 BCE), there may have been an occupation
gap or overbuilt by the Ur III rulers. Manishtushu: Fragmentary inscriptions
on black diorite vessels found at Eridu indicate he dedicated luxury objects
to the temple of Enki (E-Abzu). Naram-Sin: He is explicitly mentioned in the
Bassetki Statue inscription as receiving the "blessing" of Ea (Enki) in Eridu,
along with other major city gods, to declare himself the "God of his City."

==== Ur III period

Eridu was active during the [[ Third Dynasty of Ur ]] (22nd to 21st century
BC) and royal building activity is known from inscribed bricks, notably those
from the ziggurat of [[ Ur-Nammu ]], marked "Ur-Nammu, king of Ur, the one who
built the temple of the god Enki in Eridu."[[ ^49 ]] Three Third Dynasty
rulers designated Year Names based on the appointment of an
//en(tu)//-priestess (high priestess) of the temple of Enki in Eridu, the
highest religious office in the land at that time. In each the first two cases
it was also used as the succeeding Year Name.

- [[ Sulgi ]] Year 28 – "Year the szita-priest-who-intercedes-for-Szulgi, the son of Szulgi, the strong man, the king of Ur, the king of the four corners of the universe, was installed as en-priest of Enki in Eridu"
- [[ Amar-Sin ]] Year 8 – "Year (Ennune-kiag-Amar-Sin) Ennune-the beloved (of Amar-Sin, was installed as en-priestess of Enki in Eridu)"
- [[ Ibbi-Sin ]] Year 11 – "Year the szita-priest who prays piously for Ibbi-Sin was chosen by means of the omens as en-priest of Enki in Eridu"

<ul
  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Fired_clay_brick_stamped_with_the_name_of_Amar-Sin%2C_Ur_III%2C_from_Eridu%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg/120px-Fired_clay_brick_stamped_with_the_name_of_Amar-Sin%2C_Ur_III%2C_from_Eridu%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg" alt="Fired clay brick stamped with the name of Amar-Sin, Ur III, from Eridu, currently housed in the British Museum" height="120" width="100"

    Fired clay brick stamped with the name of Amar-Sin, Ur III, from Eridu,
    currently housed in the British Museum

In this period, at nearby Ur, a temple of Ishtar of Eridu was built by
[[ Lagash ]]'s ruler [[ Ur-Baba ]]. The Ur III ruler [[ Ur-Nammu ]]) also
recorded building a temple of Ishtar of Eridu at Ur which is assumed to have
been a rebuild.[[ ^50 ]][[ ^51 ]]

=== Middle Bronze Age

==== Isin-Larsa period

After the fall of Ur III the site was occupied and active during the
[[ Isin-Larsa ]] period (early 2nd Millennium BC) as evidenced by a Year Name
of [[ Nur-Adad ]], ruler of Larsa "Year the temple of Enki in Eridu was built"
and prior texts of Isin rulers [[ Ishbi-Erra ]] and [[ Ishme-Dagan ]] showing
control over Eridu.[[ ^52 ]] Inscribed construction bricks of Nur-Adad have
also been found at Eridu.[[ ^53 ]]

===== Lament for Eridu

The fall of early Mesopotamia cities and empires was typically believed to be
the result of falling out of favor with the gods. A genre called
[[ City Laments | city-lament ]] developed during the [[ Isin-Larsa period ]],
of which the [[ Lament for Ur ]] is the most famous. These laments had a
number of sections (kirugu) of which only fragments have been recovered. The
[[ Lament for Eridu ]] describes the fall of that city.[[ ^54 ]][[ ^55 ]]

<blockquote
  Its king stayed outside his city as if it were an alien city. He wept bitter
  tears. Father Enki stayed outside his city as if it were an alien city. He
  wept bitter tears. For the sake of his harmed city, he wept bitter tears. Its
  lady, like a flying bird, left her city. The mother of [[ E-maḫ | é-temple ]],
  holy [[ Damgalnuna ]], left her city. The divine powers of the city of holiest
  divine powers were overturned. The divine powers of the rites of the greatest
  divine powers were altered. In Eridug everything was reduced to ruin, was
  wrought with confusion.[[ ^56 ]]

==== Old Babylonian period

This continued in the [[ Old Babylonian period ]] with Hammurabi stating in
his 33rd Year Name "Year Hammu-rabi the king dug the canal (called)
'Hammu-rabi is abundance to the people', the beloved of An and Enlil,
established the everlasting waters of plentifulness for Nippur, Eridu, Ur,
Larsa, Uruk and Isin, restored Sumer and Akkad which had been scattered,
overthrew in battle the army of Mari and Malgium and caused Mari and its
territory and the various cities of Subartu to dwell under his authority in
friendship"

=== Late Bronze Age

In an inscription of [[ Kurigalzu I ]] (c. 1375 BC), a ruler of the
[[ Kassite dynasty ]] one of his epitaphs is "[he one who ke]eps the sanctuary
in Eridu in order".[[ ^57 ]]

=== Iron Age

An inscription of the
[[ Second Sealand Dynastic | list-of-kings-of-babylon#dynasty-v-2nd-sealand-10211001-bc ]]
ruler [[ Simbar-shipak ]] (c. 1021–1004 BCE) mentions a priest of
Eridu.[[ ^58 ]]

==== Neo-Assyrian period

The [[ Neo-Assyrian emperor | neo-assyrian-empire ]] [[ Sargon II ]] (722–705
BCE) awarded //andurāru//-status (described as "a periodic reinstatement of
goods and persons, alienated because of want, to their original status") to
Eridu.[[ ^59 ]]

==== Neo-Babylonian period

The Neo-Babylonian ruler [[ Nebuchadnezzar II ]] (605–562 BC) built at Eridu
as evidenced by inscribed bricks found there.[[ ^60 ]]

== Mythology

In some, but not all, versions of the [[ Sumerian King List ]], Eridu is the
first of five cities where kingship was received before a flood came over the
land. The list mentions two rulers of Eridu from the Early Dynastic period,
[[ Alulim ]] and [[ Alalngar ]].[[ ^61 ]][[ ^62 ]]

<blockquote
  //[nam]-[[ lugal ]] [[ an | anu ]]-[[ ta | ta-cuneiform ]]
  èd-dè-a-ba//
//[eri]du^^[[ ki | ki-cuneiform ]]^^
  [[ nam-lugal-la | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/𒈗#Akkadian ]]//
//eridug^^ki^^
  a₂-lu-lim lugal//
//mu 28800 i₃-ak//
//a₂-lal₃-ĝar mu 36000 i₃-ak//

Translation:

<blockquote
  When kingship from heaven was lowered,
the kingship was in Eridu
In Eridug,
  Alulim became king;
he ruled
for 28800 years.
Alaljar ruled for 36000
  years[[ ^63 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Jar_MET_ME49_133_13.jpg/250px-Jar_MET_ME49_133_13.jpg" height="322" width="250"

  <figcaption>Jar; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; 15.24 cm; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan Museum of Art

In Sumerian mythology, Eridu was the home of the [[ Abzu ]] temple of the god
[[ Enki ]], the Sumerian counterpart of the [[ Akkadian | akkadian-empire ]]
god Ea, god of deep waters, wisdom and magic. Like all the Sumerian and
Babylonian gods, Enki/Ea began as a local god who, according to the later
cosmology, came to share the rule of the cosmos with [[ Anu ]] and
[[ Enlil ]]. His kingdom was the sweet waters that lay below earth (Sumerian
//ab//=water; //zu//=far).[[ ^64 ]]
The bright star [[ Canopus ]] was known to
the ancient Mesopotamians and represented the city of Eridu in the //Three
Stars Each// [[ Babylonian star catalogues ]] and later around 1100 BC on the
[[ MUL.APIN ]] tablets.[[ ^65 ]] Canopus was called MUL.NUN^^KI^^ by the
Babylonians, which translates as "star of the city of Eridu". From most
southern city of Mesopotamia, Eridu, there is a good view to the south, so
that about 6000 years ago due to the
[[ precession of the Earth's axis | axial-precession ]] the first rising of
the star Canopus in Mesopotamia could be observed only from there at the
southern meridian at midnight. In the city of [[ Ur ]] this was the case only
60 years later.[[ ^66 ]]

In the flood myth tablet[[ ^67 ]] found in Ur, how Eridu and Alulim were
chosen by gods as first city and first priest-king is described in more
detail.[[ ^68 ]] The following is the English translation of the
tablet:[[ ^69 ]]

<blockquote
  (Obverse)
1 They (the chief deities) created humanity
2 After the
  [animals/vermin?] were proliferating below/from the earth in unison
3 They
  made livestock and quadrupeds as fitting things in the steppe(eden-na)
4 In
  the high steppe \... joyous plants broadly
5 At that time, the canal was not
  dug \...
6 The dike and ditch [were not dredged(?) \...]
7 The ox \... the
  plow/farmer/furrow(?) \...
8 The lands \... a single track \...
9 Humanity
  \... their eyes/faces \... [rain?]
10 Šakkan/Šumugan [did not go out(?)] in
  the desert \...
11 Weaving the cap/headcloth(?) \...
12 Humanity \...
13 At
  that time, the snake was not present, [the scorpion was not present \...]
14
  The lion was not present, [the hyena was not present \...]
15 The dog and wolf
  were not present \...
16 Humanity [had no opponent \...]
17 Fear and
  [gooseflesh(?) were not present \...]
18 \...
19 The king \...
20 \...

Translation:

<blockquote
  (Reverse)
1 First \... were giving [Eridu or Ku’ara?] to Asalluhi,
2 Second
  \... were giving [Bad-tibira to the nugig midwife?],
3 Third \... were giving
  [Larak[ to Pabilsaĝ,
4 Fourth \... were giving [Sippar to Utu?]
5 Fifth \...
  were giving Š[uruppak?] to [Sud?].
6 Those cities .. their settlements \...
7
  An, Enlil, [Enki?] and Ninhursaĝ
8 Among(?) those cities, Eridu .. they
  established at the front/as the leader(?)
9 They led(?) a man who was lying(?)
  among its vast and many people \...
10 An, Enlil, and Enki, the father(s) of
  the gods(?)
11 They chose Alulim/the "seed of the red deer" for the
  shepherdship of the entirety of the many people
12 They named him(!)
  Alulim/named the "seed of the red deer" \...
13 After they served according to
  the command to check thusly/forever after(?)
14 [So that?] humanity, whoever
  possessed a name, were grasping his feet (in submission)/following his
  path(?)
15 They put both (capital) offense and sin(?) in his hand
16 was
  giving \... to him
17 \...
18 \...

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Cup_MET_ME49_133_6.jpg/250px-Cup_MET_ME49_133_6.jpg" height="293" width="250"

  <figcaption>Cup; mid 6th–5th millennium BC; ceramic; 9.53 cm; Tell Abu Shahrain; Metropolitan Museum of Art

[[ Adapa ]], a man of Eridu, is depicted as an early culture hero. Although
earlier tradition, Me-Turan/Tell-Haddad tablet, describes [[ Adapa ]] as
postdiluvian ruler of Eridu,[[ ^70 ]] in late tradition, [[ Adapa ]] came to
be viewed as Alulim's vizier,[[ ^71 ]] and he was considered to have brought
civilization to the city as the sage of King [[ Alulim ]].[[ ^72 ]][[ ^73 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Statue_of_a_standing_lion_from_Eridu%2C_Iraq%2C_c._mid-3rd_millennium_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg/250px-Statue_of_a_standing_lion_from_Eridu%2C_Iraq%2C_c._mid-3rd_millennium_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg" height="388" width="250"

  <figcaption>Statue of a standing lion from Eridu, Iraq, c. mid-3rd millennium BC

The stories of [[ Inanna ]], goddess of [[ Uruk ]], describe how she had to go
to Eridu in order to receive the [[ gifts of civilization | me-mythology ]].
At first Enki, the god of Eridu, attempted to retrieve these sources of his
power but later willingly accepted that Uruk now was the centre of the
land.[[ ^74 ]][[ ^75 ]]

